    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for the Zurich Zebras prioritizes speed and situational leverage against a slate of mediocre opposing arms. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13) stands out as our strongest play, benefiting from an excellent matchup against Lowder (4.77 xERA) and favorable wind conditions that push his power-speed potential into the elite tier. While **Junior Caminero** (71.24) sits in a high-value #3 spot against Bennett, the real engine of our production remains **Trea Turner** (66.01). Despite facing a slightly tougher arm in Ginn, **Trea Turner**\u2019s elite sprint speed is boosted by Ginn\u2019s slow delivery, making him a prime candidate to steal bases\u2014a category we are aggressively hunting. **Kyle Schwarber** (61.85) rounds out the high-confidence starts, with his high xwOBA (0.392) suggesting positive regression despite a neutral platoon matchup.\n\nOn the periphery, **Caleb Durbin** (42.95) remains our most marginal start. His grade is suppressed significantly by his #9 spot in the order and the relative competence of the Jacksonville starter; however, he remains in the lineup because of his rare stolen base utility. Among our reserves, **Lars Nootbaar** and **Luke Raley** were the final cuts. **Lars Nootbaar** was under consideration due to his contact skills, but the lack of an elite platoon advantage compared to **Pete Crow-Armstrong** kept him on the pine. With **Christian Yelich** still sidelined on the IL and **Yordan Alvarez**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Kazuma Okamoto** all inactive today, our depth is being tested. We continue to monitor **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Josh Smith**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Carson Benge**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** for potential rotation updates should the late-game situational variables shift against our current starters.",
